# Apparel domain

Use this language. Do not treat a **lot** as a garment or a **vendor** as a fabric mill.

| Term | Meaning | In this app |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Article** | A fabric style. | `lots.article` |
| **Lot** | One particular textile fabric; a variant of an article (**article + color_num**). | `lots` (`lot_no`, `article`, `color_num`) |
| **Mill** | Fabric producer. Lead time up to **~90 days** for a fabric order. | Not a table yet. Not `vendors`. |
| **Model** | Detailed **shape** of a garment (belongs to an item). | `models` (`average_consumption`: optional fabric qty for one size/drop) |
| **Product** | A **lot cut into a model**. Same lot + different models = different products. Photos are paths on `product_images`; the host is `PRODUCT_IMAGE_ORIGIN` (set per deploy). Warehouse place is optional `products.location` (an aisle or a short note), edited on the inventory row. | `products` (merchandise: `lot_id` + `model_id`) |
| **Size / drop** | Variants of a **product**. | Product size/drop subset + qty cells |
| **Vendor** | Clothing **factory** that cuts garments. Usually orders the fabric needed for a cutting order. | `vendors`; `cutting_orders.vendor_id` |

## Cutting orders

Order of work: create `cutting_orders` → add fabrics to `cutting_order_lots` → add `cutting_order_products` for the **selected lot**. A product’s lot must already be on the order.

Because mill lead time is long, a cutting order is often sent to the factory **before** size/drop breakdown exists:

1. **Bulk order** (optional): first transmission — **total qty per product**, no sizes/drops. Stored as nullable `cutting_order_products.bulk_order_qty`. Null means there was no bulk send; the line may be sized only.
2. **Sized order** (what the qty grid does today): later (or only) transmission — **variable-size/drop-matrix** on `cutting_order_products`.

Bulk qty is **not binding**. The sized grid total may be higher or lower; do not constrain or overwrite one from the other.

A **size/drop scale** (`size_drop_scales`) is a reusable **percent mix** for sizing. Optional. **Apply** uses a working **qty_to_distribute** (not stored on the scale). See **scale-panel**.

Do not require size/drop qty in order to have a product on a cutting order. Do not invent mill tables or bulk-order UI until asked.

A vendor login can open **Cutting orders** (`/vendor-cuttings`) read-only: only that factory’s cuttings with a **submit date**. The panel shows each product’s ordered size/drop qty. **Print** opens **cutting-order-print**. A docked filter limits the list by cutting number, open/closed, and submit-date range. Cutting No is a Combo Box of this factory’s submitted cuttings only. **Find** jumps to the picked cutting when it is in the current list. Staff keep `/cutting-orders`.

## Vendor shipments (receiving)

Goods the **vendor** ships to us. One airway bill can include **several cuttings**. The same **product** may appear once per cutting on that shipment.

1. `vendor_shipments` — `shipment_num` (serial), `vendor_id` (factory), `received_date`, `air_way_no`, `posted` (add to inventory). Do not change vendor once the shipment has product lines.
2. `vendor_shipment_products` — `cutting_order_id` + `product_id` (must already be on that cutting). Unique `(shipment, cutting, product)`.
3. `vendor_shipment_cells` — size/drop qty. **Post** adds to `inventory` and to `cutting_quantities.qty_received`. Refuse the whole post (no writes) if any line’s cutting is closed or that cutting product is Complete. No unpost unless asked. After `qty_received` changes, if that product’s sized **received ≥ ordered** (and ordered > 0), `cutting_order_products.status` becomes Complete. Does not auto-reopen. The existing product-status rollup still closes the cutting order when no products remain Open.

## Vendor advice

The UI calls this a **vendor packing list**. The tables stay `vendor_advices`, `vendor_advice_products`, and `vendor_advice_cells` (draft → submitted). Not a vendor shipment. Staff **Accept** copies it into an unposted `vendor_shipments` row. **Return** sets it back to draft. Accept and return do not post inventory or change `qty_received`. See **vendor-advice**.

## Order requests

Customers do not write `customer_orders`. They shop at `/shop` and keep drafts at `/my-orders`. Tables: `customer_order_requests`, `customer_order_request_products`, `customer_order_request_cells` (migration 0057). Status is draft, submitted, accepted. The header has the same season, dates, addresses, terms, shipping, and transmission fields as a customer order so a later sales-rep request can fill them the way **Add new order** does. This pass only fills season, dates, addresses, and notes, and copies the customer's sales rep. Price on a line is the catalog price. **Accept** into a pending customer order is not built yet.

**Shop** is a Fit List of published active merchandise, each row the same photo thumbnail as inventory (hover the full photo, click the gallery), plus the name, price, and a description with no labels: brand, item, pattern, color, and content. Availability sits on its own line in red. The list takes two thirds of the row. **Sizes** sits to the left of **Add to cart** and opens a popup of the **available-sizes-matrix** for that product. **Add to cart** is **sys_image** `add-to-cart.svg` (`public/sys_image/add-to-cart.svg`, `SysAddToCart`). It keeps the product in the browser. A product already in the cart shows the same image faded, and the button does nothing. **Cart** is a container to the right of that list, one third of the row, with the same border and a very light amber fill (`bg-[#f8f5e8]`). Its header is an amber bar in the same gradient, gold edge, and gloss as **sys_image** `cart.svg`, with that image’s **Cart** word and cart mark on the left. The bar’s corners match the container (`rounded-lg`). A count sits on the bar while it has products. Each line is a white row with the product name, the price, and **×** to remove it. An empty cart reads **The cart is empty.** **New order** and, when the customer has a draft, **Open order** and **Add to this order** stay at the bottom. **New order** creates a draft and empties the cart. **Add to this order** adds the cart onto a draft, skipping a product already on it, then empties the cart. Both open that order.

**available-sizes-matrix** is the inventory size/drop grid with no quantities and no totals. A cell with on-hand quantity above zero shows a green box. An empty cell stays empty. A catalog upcharge above zero is centered in that cell as `+$` currency, in red, at 85% of the cell type. When that cell is also on hand, the amount stays centered and the cell is green. The shop popup loads it when **Sizes** is clicked and learns only which cells are available, plus those extras. It has a close **×**.

**My orders** is fit-list+details. The customer sees **Order {n}**, date, and Draft, Sent, or Accepted. **Send** submits a draft. **Edit** changes season, dates, addresses, and notes. **Delete** only while draft. The folder opens a right dock of the products. There is no **Add a product**. A draft line edits the size/drop matrix or deletes the product. Sent and accepted lines are read-only.

## Customers

`customers.account_no` is the account serial, assigned when the customer is created and not edited. `account_number` is the Sage account. `customers.company_name` is the name on lists. `business_name` is the legal or invoice name, and a blank one is saved as the company name. `payment_term_id` points at `payment_terms` (`term`, `description`, `days_due`). `sales_rep_id` is the sales rep who services that customer, a `profiles` row with role sales rep. Blank is allowed. Creating an order copies that rep onto `customer_orders.sales_rep_id`. Later edits to the customer's rep leave existing orders as they were. Saving an order header copies the customer's current rep onto that order. `do_not_ship` defaults to false and does not block an order or a shipment. `notes` is optional. **Filter** beside the page title opens a docked filter. Account, name, business name, Sage account, email, phone, and cell phone match contains. Terms, sales rep, status, and do not ship are Combo Boxes. **Find** on Account or Name jumps to that customer in the filtered list and closes the panel. On the Customers Fit List, **Add new customer** sits above the list and opens a card with every customer field. **Edit** and **Delete** are on the customer details panel. **Edit** opens the same card. Each card has a close **×** and a transparent overlay. Each text field and Combo Box has a clear **×** inside on the right. Notes puts that **×** at the top right. The checkboxes have no **×**. **Save** and **Cancel** appear only after a field changes. The row stays one line and shows Account, Name, Business name, Sales rep, and Status. A column with no header shows **Do not ship** in red only when that flag is on. On the details panel, a red-and-white stop sign sits after Status when that flag is on. Sage, sales rep, contact, email, phone, cell phone, terms, and notes are on the customer details panel to the right of the list, for the selected customer. Addresses on that panel are a view-only Fit List: location name, type, and the combined address. **Addresses**, with the folder icon, opens a docked panel on the right. If an address is selected in the details list, the panel list opens on that address. **Add**, **Edit**, and **.....** each open a card over a transparent overlay, with a close **×**. **.....** is read-only. **Save** and **Cancel** appear only after a field changes. **Delete** stays on the address list header for the selected address.

Creating a customer order stores the term chosen on the card in `customer_orders.terms`. Picking a customer fills that Combo Box with the customer's term, and it can be changed before save. Later edits to the customer's term leave existing orders as they were.

## Customer orders

A customer order is three levels, the same shape as a customer shipment. Staff page `/customer-orders` (admin, warehouse, sales rep). Not a customer portal. **Add new order** sits above the list and opens a card. **Notes** is the last field on that card, with a clear **×** at the top right. Under it, the orders Fit List on the left and **Order details** on the right, each 50% wide, both with the Fit List box chrome. Order details shows the selected order: a header of customer name **· Order No {n}** with **Open**, **Print**, **Approve**, **Revoke**, **Edit**, and **Delete** on one line, aligned with each other. The icons have no box around them. A thin divider follows them, then **Edit** and **Delete** sit in the same boxed chip as a Fit List row (`RowButtons`). Open and Print are icons that fill that header’s height. **Approve** is **sys_image** `approve.svg` (`public/sys_image/approve.svg`), scaled to that header’s height. It shows only while the order is pending, and only while every open merchandise line is still pending. Clicking it opens a card: **Approve** writes the status, **Cancel** leaves the order pending. Once the order is approved, that spot shows **sys_image** `approved_Stamp.svg` (`public/sys_image/approved_Stamp.svg`) at the same height, and **Revoke** (`public/sys_image/revoke.svg`) beside it. **Revoke** sets the order back to pending. **Edit** opens the same card as **Add new order**, filled with this order. **Terms** is a Combo Box above Shipping method on the new-order card and the edit card. **Save** and **Cancel** appear only after a field changes. The list rows do not edit. Under the header, season and cancel date sit first with no group title, then **Customer** (account, Sage, sales rep, entered by) and **Terms and shipping** (terms, received via, shipping method). **Ship to** and **Bill to** sit side by side, then notes. Each of these is a bordered stone-50 group. Every title (Customer, Terms and shipping, Ship to, Bill to, Notes) is a highlighted block header across the top of its group: stone-200 bar, bottom border, semibold text. The season group has no title and no bar. Text is compact label and value: season and cancel date in two columns, Customer in four, Terms and shipping in three. An **Order summary** group is pinned under the scrolling details, with the same rows as the report: one row per item (units, item, amount), **Total quantity**, **Surcharges**, **Freight**, **Grand total**, **Shipped**. Clicking it opens the docked products panel, the same as the folder, and the pointer is a link. `summarizeOrder` (`src/lib/order-summary.ts`) adds it up for this group, the report, and the PDF, and canceled lines are left out. Date, status, left to ship, start, and the order number stay on the list and are left out here. Nothing selected reads **Select an order.** List rows have no buttons; the order number stays a link. The folder opens a docked products panel on the right, 95% wide. Its title is one line: the customer name, then **· Order No {n}**. There is no lots list. **Add a product** is **products-button-list-picker**. **Charges** sits to the left of **Any**, then the in-set items. **Any** and the item buttons each open a Combo of active merchandise that is not already on the order. **Charges** opens a Combo of active surcharge and freight that are not already on the order. Freight sets a cost and the quantity is always 1. A surcharge sets a quantity and a price. Both start from the product’s standard price. **Inventory**, a switch on **Any** and each item popup, is off each time that popup opens. Off, price and size extras show and **Add** writes the line. On, those are hidden and **Inventory** opens the inventory window under the picker bar and leaves the popup open. That list then includes products already on the order. Selecting or editing a row leaves that window open. It closes on its ×, when the products panel closes, when the page is left, or when another inventory window opens. Only one inventory window is open at a time. **Add** writes the line. A charge row has no size/drop matrix and no dollar button. Double-click **blue freight** or **blue surcharge** edits that row. The pointer is a link, and the title reads **Double click to edit**. Freight shows the cost and **sys_image** **blue freight** (`public/sys_image/blue-freight.svg`, 56px). A surcharge shows quantity and price, and both can be edited, with **sys_image** **blue surcharge** (`public/sys_image/blue-surcharge.svg`, 56px). The extended price is quantity times price. Freight’s extended price is the cost. Edit and Delete stay. After a product is chosen, its price and size/drop extras can be edited for this order before **Add**. The product catalog is left as it is. Each product row shows products.name and that price. The name and price take 16% of the row. The list sorts by product type, then product name. In edit mode a dollar button opens a card for that line’s price and size extras. **Save** writes those and leaves the quantities as they are. **Save** and **Cancel** appear only after a field changes. The card has a close **×** and a transparent overlay. A new line’s size/drop matrix is one blank row. **Inventory** and **Copy** sit in the Edit and Delete box on a merchandise row, on that same line. **Copy** opens **copy-list** for that order. **Inventory** opens a non-modal popup (no overlay) just below its row (above when there is no room), never over it, so the row can be edited without moving the window. It has a close **×**. Its title bar is a drag handle (pointer `move`, kept inside the window), and once moved it stays where the user put it. Under the title, a thin line (`font-light`) shows brand, color, pattern, and content from the product's lot. **On hand** is a read-only size/drop matrix of `inventory.qty_on_hand` with a total, or "None on hand." **Arrives next** sits at the bottom. When a cutting is coming it is a button; clicking it extends the window and shows that cutting's ordered size/drop matrix, read-only, and clicking again folds it. The cutting is the soonest arrival of a submitted, open cutting that still has that product open, today or later; a shared date uses the lower cutting number. No date shows — and is not a button. It loads on hand when opened. `loadProductInventory` reads inventory, the lot description, and calls `product_next_arrival` (migration 0056, readable by every staff role, returns one date). The matrix calls `product_next_cutting` (migration 0057, staff only). While it is open, clicking its selected row does not deselect it (`SelectableRow keepSelected`). Only one is open at a time. It closes with **×**, a second click on **Inventory**, selecting another row (or none), when the products panel closes, or when the page is left. Freight and surcharge have no Copy and no Inventory. Double-click that matrix to edit the row. The pointer is a link, and the title reads **Double click to edit**. The extended price sits at the bottom of the row, in a box the same width as the grand total amounts and in that column. The row grows when the line needs the room. It is the sum of each size/drop quantity times the line price plus its size extra, shown as currency with no label. A zero total shows `$0.00`. Under the list, saved amounts for open lines add up as freight, surcharge, merchandise, and the total extended price. Canceled lines are left out. Amber **+$** appears on a row’s size cells only after that row’s drop is set. Drops with a quantity stay on the matrix. The order number still opens the order page. The card has a close **×** and a transparent overlay. **Save** and **Cancel** appear only after a field changes. Customer, bill-to, ship-to, shipping method, received via, start date, and cancel date each have a clear **×** inside on the right. Bill-to lists billing and both. Ship-to lists shipping and both. Each option reads location name, then city and street: Store • New York, 111 21st Street. Choosing a both address for bill-to also sets ship-to to that address. Ship-to can be changed afterward. A billing-only choice, or clearing bill-to, leaves ship-to as it is. Season sits above start and cancel. Season is a **Ship at once** checkbox. When it is off, a season Combo Box appears and has a clear **×**. The checkbox has no **×**. Checking **Ship at once** stores that name, hides the season list, and sets the start date to today. The start date can still be changed. Unchecking **Ship at once** leaves the start date as it is and clears the season. Start and cancel sit on one line. `cancel_date` is the latest date the order can be delivered. After that date the order is canceled.

1. `customer_orders` — `order_no` (serial), `created_at` (shown as **Date** after Order on the list, date part only, not edited), customer, status, dates. The list columns are Order, Date, Customer, Start, Status, Left to ship; no account or Sage columns. **Approved** status text is red. Row data: ship-to and bill-to copied from the address book, rep, who entered it. `future_season` is **Ship at once** or a season name. `shipment_method_id` points at `shipment_methods` (`shipper`, `method`; Pickup has no shipper). `transmission_method_id` points at `transmission_methods` (`name`: how the order was received). Saving the order copies the chosen names into `shipping_method` and `transmission_method`. Later edits to a lookup row leave existing orders as they were until that order is saved again. Commission rates are not on these lists yet.
2. `customer_order_products` — one product once. `price` is the negotiated line price (starts from the product price). `qty` is the surcharge quantity. Freight stores 1. Merchandise leaves `qty` null and uses size/drop cells. `commission_rate` is a fraction (`0.1000` is 10%). `status` is the line: pending, back order, short shipped, complete, canceled.
3. `customer_order_cells` — size/drop `qty`, `qty_shipped` (starts at 0), and optional `price`. A blank cell price means use the line price. Adding a product copies `product_price_upcharges` onto those cells. Later catalog edits do not rewrite the order.

Header status is pending, approved, picking, back order, short shipped, complete, or canceled. Approved and picking are set by staff while every open merchandise line is still pending. The other header statuses roll up from merchandise lines (product type 1). Freight and surcharge lines do not move the header. An order with no merchandise keeps its header. On the customer orders list, **Filter** beside the page title opens a docked filter. Order no matches contains, and **Find** jumps to that order in the filtered list. Customer, season, and open or complete are Combo Boxes. Season lists **Ship at once**, then the seasons, and keeps orders whose `future_season` equals the pick. Open keeps pending, approved, picking, back order, and short shipped. Complete keeps complete. A blank value keeps every order, including canceled. Left to ship is a whole number and matches that quantity. **Products**, at the bottom, is a Combo of active merchandise (plus a product that is on an order and no longer active). **Add** puts the pick on a list; each row has a **×**. **Filter** keeps an order that has a line for any listed product, including a canceled line. An empty list is ignored. Start from and start to limit the start date. **Sort by left to ship** sits left of **Filter**: ↑ low to high, ↓ high to low, × back to order number. Ties keep the newest order first. **Left to ship** is the merchandise ordered quantity minus the quantity shipped. It is 0 when the order is complete or canceled. Freight and surcharge are left out. On a merchandise row, shipped of ordered sits after the line status, as `0 of 14`. **Print** on Order details opens **customer-order-print** in a new tab. A customer shipment does not write `qty_shipped` yet.
